GCP:

Workload identity-
Before workload identity, GKE workloads typically authenticated to GCP using IAM Service account json keys stored as k8s secrets or by using
the node's service account through the metadata server. Both approaches had security drawbacks such as long lived credentials and overlay broad
permissions.
Workload identity solved this by mapping k8s service accounts to IAM identities and issuing short-lived credentials without storing keys.

KSA(kubernetes service account)

    apiVersion: v1
    kind: ServiceAccount
    metadata:
      name: secret-reader
      namespace: prod

Pod

    apiVersion: v1
    kind: Pod
    metadata:
      name: api-app
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: secret-reader
      containers:
      - name: app
        image: us-docker-pkg.dev/project/api:v1


This pod runs as

    prod/secret-reader

To allow the pod to read secrets from secret manager-

create gcp service account
    
    gcloud iam service-accounts create secret-reader-gsa

    output: secret-reader-gsa@my-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com

grant secret manager permission

    gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding my-project \
        --member="serviceAccount:secret-reader-gsa@my-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com" \
        --role="roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor"

Now the GSA can read secrets.

Bind KSA to GSA

    gcloud iam service-accounts add-iam-policy-binding \
    secret-reader-gsa@my-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
    --role roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser \
    --member \
    "serviceAccount:my-project.svc.id.goog[prod/secret-reader]"

Annotate KSA

    kubectl annotate serviceaccount secret-reader \
    iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account=\
    secret-reader-gsa@my-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com

Runtime flow

pod-->KSA token-->GKE metadata server-->google sts-->temporary OAuth token-->secret manager API

No json key exists. Google issues short-lived creds automatically.

